  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule. The rule is enabled and its first run succeeded with no alerts.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view (`Kibana Sample Data Logs`, time field `timestamp`) has one document per web request. The fields I built on:\n- `response.keyword` (HTTP status code)\n- `clientip` (visitor address)\n- `bytes` (response size)\n- `url.keyword`, `request.keyword` and `extension.keyword` (what was requested)\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest` (origin and destination country)\n- `machine.os.keyword` and `agent.keyword` (client details)\n- `referer` and `tags` (other context)\n- `hour_of_day` (a field calculated from the timestamp)\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-041332e1 Web logs overview\"** (id `4ad03aad-ad21-45dc-aad8-5d3b1eebf73a`). It has 8 panels, all using the existing data view, and shows the last 7 days by default:\n- **Four headline numbers:** total requests, unique visitors (distinct `clientip`), average response size, and 5xx error count.\n- **Requests over time by response code:** stacked bar chart.\n- **Requests by response code:** pie chart.\n- **Top 10 requested URLs:** table with request count and total bytes.\n- **Top 10 source countries:** horizontal bar chart.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-041332e1 5xx spike\"** (id `a950d277-ee02-4720-9efc-fb1a2b8a5377`). It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that runs every minute. It counts requests with a response code from 500 to 599 over the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is above 10.\n\n- **Comparing codes as text:** the response code is stored as text, so the 500\u2013599 check compares strings rather than numbers. That works only because every code is three digits.\n- **No notifications yet:** the rule has no actions attached. When it fires, the alert shows up in Kibana, but no email or other message goes out until you add a connector.",
